// case: generic-union-needs-a-written-type-for-none
// what: `union Option[T]` is legal and `Option.Some(21)` fixes the argument from
//      the payload it carries. `Option.None` carries nothing, so no value says
//      which instantiation it is. The argument may only come from a position
//      where a type is WRITTEN -- a binding's annotation, a `return` in a
//      `-> Option<int>` function, a call argument against its parameter -- and
//      here none of them is: `val x` has no annotation. Guessing `Option_int`
//      from the first row that declares `None` is the trade this repository
//      already refused for generic models, so the construction is refused.
// expect-error: carries no payload, so nothing in this expression says which instantiation
union Option[T] {
    Some(T)
    None
}

fn main() -> int {
    val x = Option.None
    return 0
}
